From: Petr Wozniak <petr.wozniak@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] map-controller: cut the children loose before freeing a device

A node ageing out leaves every node behind it holding two pointers into
freed memory.

struct backhaul_info keeps bh.parent as a pointer to another
bh_topology_dev, and bh.parent_iface as a pointer into that device's
ifaces[] array. topology_remove_device() frees the device and leaves both
pointers standing in every child. This is not an exotic path: it is called
from cntlr_clean_node(), i.e. whenever a node ages out.

topology->valid is set false here for exactly this hazard, and topology.h
says reading backhaul info while it is false is undefined. But only one
place in the controller asks - cntlr.c, is_topology_valid(). The parent
chain walk, the neighbour match, and the ubus topology dump all guard with

	if (dev->bh.parent)
		... dev->bh.parent->al_macaddr ...

and a freed pointer passes that test. The safety flag protects one reader
out of four.

So the pointers are cleared instead of being left to a flag nobody reads.
The NULL checks that already exist then mean what they say, and a child of a
departed node reads as having no parent - which is the truth - rather than
reading freed memory. Same shape as the policy binding: make the guard the
code already has become effective, instead of adding a second one.

own_iface is not touched: it points into the device's own ifaces[], so it
dies with the device and no one else holds it.

Verify: with a three-level mesh, power off the middle node and let it age
out. `ubus call map.controller topology` must show the orphan without a
parent instead of a random address, and must not fault.

Signed-off-by: Petr Wozniak <petr.wozniak@gmail.com>
---
--- a/src/topology.c
+++ b/src/topology.c
@@ -209,6 +209,39 @@
 
 	list_for_each_entry_safe(bh_topo_dev, bh_topo_dev_tmp, &topology->dev_list, list) {
 		if (hwaddr_equal(bh_topo_dev->al_macaddr, al_macaddr)) {
+			struct bh_topology_dev *other = NULL;
+
+			/* Cut the children loose before freeing what they point at.
+			 *
+			 * bh.parent points at another device and bh.parent_iface
+			 * into that device's ifaces[] array, so freeing a device
+			 * leaves every child of it holding two pointers into freed
+			 * memory - and this runs on ordinary node ageout, not on
+			 * anything exotic.
+			 *
+			 * topology->valid is set false here for exactly this
+			 * reason, and the header says reading backhaul info is
+			 * undefined while it is false. But only one reader in the
+			 * controller asks (cntlr.c: is_topology_valid). The others -
+			 * the parent-chain walk, the neighbour match, the ubus
+			 * topology dump - guard with `if (dev->bh.parent)` and
+			 * dereference. A freed pointer passes that test.
+			 *
+			 * So the pointers are cleared rather than left for a flag
+			 * nobody reads: the NULL checks that already exist then
+			 * mean what they say, and a child of a departed node reads
+			 * as having no parent instead of reading freed memory.
+			 */
+			list_for_each_entry(other, &topology->dev_list, list) {
+				if (other == bh_topo_dev)
+					continue;
+				if (other->bh.parent != bh_topo_dev)
+					continue;
+
+				other->bh.parent = NULL;
+				other->bh.parent_iface = NULL;
+			}
+
 			list_del(&bh_topo_dev->list);
 			free(bh_topo_dev);
 
